  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \n\nRecent cooler temperatures will transition to more summer heat and \nhumidly today as surface high pressure shifts east of the area. \nWidespread low clouds and fog will overspread the area and continue \nthru the morning before improving. \n\nWarm and humid air returns on southerly flow west of the departing \nhigh to start the week while upper level northwesterly flow persists.\n\nA couple periods of showers and storms are possible to start the \nweek. The first chance for showers and storms is tonight. A cluster \nof storms/MCS may form in the Great Lakes region this evening diving \nsoutheast late tonight. The best axis of instability remains to our \nwest which would likely favor any storms that do form diving south \ninto Indiana and Illinois. \n\nHowever, the presence of some instability over our area allows \na low end chance for precipitation. The severe risk with any storms \ntonight is marginal since instability is limited and elevated. \n\nThe second, and much better threat for storms is on Monday and \nMonday night when clusters of storms/MCSs may form to our northwest \nahead of an approaching cold front before diving south/southeast \ninto the Ohio Valley. \n\nDetails on placement continues to be highly uncertain since the \nbackground environment over the region will likely be characterized \nby moderate to high instability and sufficient shear for organized \nconvection with little in the way of organized forcing. \n\nMesoscale interactions will have large impacts on how Monday/Monday \nnight plays out. At the time, the most likely timing for storms over \nthe Ohio Valley is Monday night. A damaging wind threat exists \ngiven the potential for organized clusters of storms/MCSs Monday into\nMonday night.\n\nAlso, hot and humid conditions on Monday could lead to the heat \nindex reaching the 95 to 100 degree range if convection does not \noccur early in the day.",
